// spelling_keyed — the recurring defect of this codebase, as a rule.
//
// A PLACE WALKER decomposes `a.b`, `t.0`, `v[i]`, `*p` down to a root. Such a
// function must handle every PROJECTION, because the property is "a step from a
// place to a sub-place" and the property does not care how the step is spelled.
// Eight times in one week a walker enumerated the spellings its author had in
// mind and silently declined the rest:
//   `try_path`               VarRef|FieldRead, missing TupleIndex/Deref/IndexRead
//                            -> two mut closures over `t.0` did not conflict, and
//                               one aliased at runtime
//   `extract_borrow_place`   no TupleIndex arm -> `&x.0` recorded NO loan at all
//   `is_temporary_value_expr` listed literals and calls, missing operators
//                            -> `return &(2+3);` returned a dead frame
// None of these is findable by grep, because the defect is an ABSENCE. It is
// findable by a rule, because an absence over a finite domain is derivable.
// THE DOMAIN IS DERIVED, NOT LISTED. expr_code comes from the schema enum;
// not_projection is the claim. Subtracting means a NEW expr code is a
// projection until someone exempts it — loud by default, because the opposite
// default is exactly the blindness these rules exist to detect.
.decl expr_code(k: symbol)
.decl not_projection(k: symbol)
.decl projection_kind(k: symbol)
.decl place_root_kind(k: symbol)
.decl walker(f: symbol)
.decl handles(f: symbol, k: symbol)
.input expr_code
.input not_projection
.output projection_kind
projection_kind(K) :- expr_code(K), !not_projection(K).
.input place_root_kind
.input walker
.input handles

// A walker that handles SOME projections but not all of them is keyed on the
// spelling its author enumerated, not on the property.
.decl spelling_keyed(f: symbol, missing: symbol)
.output spelling_keyed
spelling_keyed(F, K) :- walker(F), projection_kind(K), !handles(F, K),
                        projection_kind(K2), handles(F, K2).

// A walker that terminates on no root at all is not walking anything — that is
// a different defect and must not be reported as the one above.
.decl no_root(f: symbol)
.output no_root
no_root(F) :- walker(F), !any_root(F).
.decl any_root(f: symbol)
any_root(F) :- place_root_kind(K), handles(F, K).

// How complete each walker is, so the report is a measurement and not a verdict.
.decl coverage(f: symbol, have: number, want: number)
.output coverage
coverage(F, H, W) :- walker(F),
    H = count : { projection_kind(K), handles(F, K) },
    W = count : { projection_kind(_K2) }.
